 "vyzhimka": {
  "tekst": "I found enough to identify the people behind Business Intelligence DNA, but I’d distinguish between “a real business” and “an established business with a verifiable track record.”\n\nBusiness Intelligence DNA says it is a DBA/brand of Wealthboosterpro LLC, a Wyoming limited-liability company. Its own current terms identify the two people personally doing the consulting as Andrii Zhyla, responsible for strategy and positioning, and Maryna Zhyla, responsible for business economics and described as a former CFO. The site explicitly says there are only two of them and no employees. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms))\n\nThere is independent evidence that these are real people with a business history. Maryna Zhyla has a LinkedIn profile identifying her with “Wealthboosterpro Corp.” and describing herself as an entrepreneur in the Los Angeles area. ([linkedin.com](https://www.linkedin.com/in/marinazhyla?utm_source=openai)) Andrii Zhyla appears in multiple independent corporate records. For example, California corporate data identifies him as CEO, CFO, director and registered agent of an active California company called Da Films Corp., incorporated in 2021. ([bizprofile.net](https://www.bizprofile.net/ca/santa-monica/da-films-corp?utm_source=openai)) UK Companies House records also show an Andrii Zhyla as a former person with significant control of J.B. Finance LLP. ([find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk](https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/OC399405/filing-history?utm_source=openai)) There are older records connecting Andrii and Maryna Zhyla together as principals of A.M. Bizz LLC in Florida. ([florida.intercreditreport.com](https://florida.intercreditreport.com/company/a-m-bizz-llc-l18000151698?utm_source=openai))\n\nThere are, however, some reasons I would treat Business Intelligence DNA as a very new operation rather than an established consulting firm:\n\n- The domain businessinteldna.com appears in a list of .com domains newly registered on September 6, 2026 — only 10 days ago as of September 16, 2026. ([com.all-url.info](https://com.all-url.info/21/25/?utm_source=openai))\n- Its legal documents are correspondingly brand new: the contact page is dated September 7, the privacy policy became effective September 8, and the current terms became effective September 14, 2026. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/contacts))\n- The Casper address, 5830 E 2nd St Ste 7000, should not be interpreted as evidence of a staffed Business Intelligence DNA office. Government documents show Republic Registered Agent LLC using exactly that address as a Wyoming registered-agent address for numerous unrelated companies. ([sec.gov](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1945868/000194586823000006/exhibit_d_23.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n- I found essentially no independent reviews, press coverage, client case studies, or other established web footprint specifically for “Business Intelligence DNA.” Given that the domain itself appears to be about 10 days old, that's not necessarily suspicious, but it means there isn't much independent evidence yet by which to judge the service.\n\nOne thing in their favor is that the website is unusually specific about who operates it, pricing, contractual terms, refunds/limitations, data handling, and the third-party systems it uses. For example, it explicitly identifies Netlify, Anthropic, Google, ElevenLabs, Twilio, Stripe, Calendly, Zoom and others and describes what customer information each receives. ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/privacy)) Their terms also spell out actual prices ($500 diagnostics, implementation starting at $5,000, and AI-agent products starting at $1,000 setup) and identify Andrii and Maryna rather than hiding behind an anonymous “team.” ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/terms)) Those are positive legitimacy signals, although they don't prove quality or successful delivery.\n\nSo my assessment is: Business Intelligence DNA appears to be a real, newly launched two-person business operated by Andrii and Maryna Zhyla, rather than an obviously fabricated company. I did not find evidence that would justify calling it a scam. But I also would not describe it as a well-established company on the evidence currently available. The Business Intelligence DNA brand/site is extremely new, its Wyoming address is a registered-agent/virtual business address, and its claimed capabilities and results don't yet have much independent verification.\n\nIf you're considering paying them $500–$5,000+, I can do a considerably deeper due-diligence check — including the actual Wyoming registration for Wealthboosterpro LLC, Andrii and Maryna's business history, lawsuits/complaints, prior companies, domain/website history, and whether their claimed experience can be independently corroborated.",
